{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"parameter-passing","title":"Pass by Value, Reference & Pointer","group":"Functions","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">How a parameter is declared decides whether the function gets a copy, an alias to the caller's object, or its address.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch-3\"></i>The three ways</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nvoid byValue(int x)      { x = 100; std::cout &lt;&lt; \"inside \" &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }  // a copy\nvoid byReference(int&amp; x) { x = 200; }        // x IS the caller's variable\nvoid byPointer(int* x)   { if (x) *x = 300; } // modifies through the address (may be null)\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 1;\n    byValue(a);      std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    byReference(a);  std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    byPointer(&amp;a);   std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    byPointer(nullptr);                       // allowed: pointers can express “no object”\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>inside 100\n1\n200\n300</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Copies?</th><th>Can modify caller?</th><th>Can be null?</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T x</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>—</td><td>Cheap types (<code>int</code>, <code>double</code>, small structs, iterators), or when you need a copy anyway</td></tr><tr><td><code>const T&amp; x</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><strong>Read-only</strong> access to anything non-trivial (<code>std::string</code>, <code>std::vector</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>T&amp; x</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Out/in-out parameters</td></tr><tr><td><code>T* x</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Optional objects, C APIs</td></tr><tr><td><code>T&amp;&amp; x</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes (steals)</td><td>No</td><td>Taking ownership of temporaries (move semantics)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-gauge\"></i>Why <code>const&amp;</code> for big objects</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstruct Tracker {\n    std::string name;\n    Tracker(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {}\n    Tracker(const Tracker&amp; other) : name(other.name) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy!\\n\"; }\n};\n\nvoid slow(Tracker t)        { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"slow \" &lt;&lt; t.name &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\nvoid fast(const Tracker&amp; t) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"fast \" &lt;&lt; t.name &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n\nint main() {\n    Tracker big(\"report\");\n    slow(big);      // copies the whole object\n    fast(big);      // no copy\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>copy!\nslow report\nfast report</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i>Swapping — the classic example</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nvoid swapRef(int&amp; a, int&amp; b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }\nvoid swapPtr(int* a, int* b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }\nint main() {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    swapRef(x, y);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    swapPtr(&amp;x, &amp;y);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>21\n12</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Passing arrays and containers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint sumArray(const int* arr, std::size_t n) {   // a C array decays to a pointer: size is lost\n    int s = 0;\n    for (std::size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) s += arr[i];\n    return s;\n}\nvoid doubleAll(std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; v) { for (int&amp; x : v) x *= 2; }\n\nint main() {\n    int raw[] = {1, 2, 3};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sumArray(raw, 3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 2, 3};\n    doubleAll(v);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v[0] &lt;&lt; v[1] &lt;&lt; v[2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6\n246</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Guideline</div><p>Rule of thumb: <strong>in</strong> params — by value if cheap to copy (≤ ~2 words), else <code>const T&amp;</code>. <strong>Out/in-out</strong> — <code>T&amp;</code>. <strong>Optional</strong> — <code>T*</code> or <code>std::optional</code>. <strong>Sink</strong> params you'll store — by value, then <code>std::move</code> into place.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A non-const reference can't bind to a temporary: <code>void f(std::string&amp; s); f(\"hi\");</code> is an error. <code>const std::string&amp;</code> can — the temporary lives until the call returns.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is Java/Python-style pass by reference the same as C++ references?</summary><div><p>No. Java and Python pass a <em>reference value</em> by value: reassigning the parameter doesn't affect the caller. A C++ <code>T&amp;</code> parameter is a true alias: <code>x = other;</code> inside the function assigns to the caller's object.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you pass by pointer instead of reference?</summary><div><p>When “no object” is a valid input (<code>nullptr</code>), when interoperating with C APIs, or when you want the call site to show that the argument may be modified (<code>update(&amp;config)</code>). Otherwise references are safer: they can't be null or re-seated.</p></div></details></section>"}